- socket-intercept-unconditional-logging: every intercepted libc call in
  `socket-intercept.c` (socket, listen, bind, connect, accept, send*,
  recv*, read/write/readv/writev, getsockopt, setsockopt, close,
  shutdown, fcntl, poll, ...) unconditionally logs a line via
  `buffer_puts(&o, ...)` with no verbosity/debug flag gating it -- there's
  no way to load this LD_PRELOAD shim without full per-call logging.
  `intercept_init()` only lets `SOCKET_INTERCEPT_LOG` redirect where the
  log goes (file vs. stderr fallback), never whether it happens at all.
  Should have an on/off (or level) check, e.g. another env var, so the
  shim can be used for its actual interception behavior without forcing
  a log line (with buffer contents via `fmt_buf`/`fmt_sockaddr`) for
  every single socket operation.

- byte-equal-notimingattack-unimplemented: `byte_equal_notimingattack()`
  is declared in `lib/byte.h` (with `__pure__`, no less) but has no
  implementation anywhere in the tree -- no
  `lib/byte/byte_equal_notimingattack.c`, no inline definition. Any
  caller would fail to link. Found while writing `tests/test_byte.c`;
  left that test as an empty stub rather than calling a function that
  doesn't exist.

- array-shift-chop-unimplemented: `array_shift()` and `array_chop()` are
  declared in `lib/array.h` but have no implementation anywhere in the
  tree (no `lib/array/array_shift.c` or `array_chop.c`). Any caller
  would fail to link. Found while writing `tests/test_array.c`; left
  those tests as empty stubs.

- buffer-mmapshared-unimplemented: `buffer_mmapshared()` (the
  filename-taking variant) has no implementation anywhere in the tree --
  only `buffer_mmapshared_fd` exists. Its siblings
  `buffer_mmapread`/`buffer_mmapprivate` both have a real filename-based
  implementation (open + call the `_fd` form, or call
  `mmap_read`/`mmap_private` directly) in addition to their `_fd`
  counterpart; `buffer_mmapshared` is missing that half. Any caller
  would fail to link. Found while writing `tests/test_buffer.c`; left
  that test as an empty stub.

- path-basename-empty-for-no-separator: `path_basename("path")` (no
  separator anywhere in the input) returns `""` instead of `"path"`.
  Root cause in `lib/path/path_basepos2.c`: it computes `n =
  u8b_rchrs(x, len, PATHSEP_S_MIXED, ...)`, and `u8b_rchrs()` returns
  `len` specifically to mean "no separator found" (same convention as
  `byte_rchr`). `path_basepos2()`'s very first check, `if(n == len)
  return n;`, treats that "not found" sentinel as if it were a valid
  position, returning an index one past the end of the string (the
  terminating NUL) instead of 0 (the whole string is the basename when
  there's no separator). Confirmed via `tests/test_path.c`'s
  `test_path_basename`.

- path-skips-resets-position: `path_skips()` in `lib/path/path_skips.c`
  is supposed to skip one path component (a run of non-separator chars)
  plus its trailing separator run, cumulatively -- e.g. repeated calls
  over `"//blah/la/lumpur"` should land at offsets 2, 7, 10. Instead it
  always returns 0 unless the string starts with a separator, and even
  then only ever returns the length of a single leading separator run.
  Root cause: the function has two back-to-back for-loops -- the first
  correctly advances `i` past the non-separator "name" part, but the
  second loop starts with `for(i = 0; ...)`, resetting `i` back to 0 and
  discarding everything the first loop found, so only the second loop's
  separator-skipping ever has any effect. There's a correct, simpler
  commented-out implementation directly below it in the same file (skip
  non-separators, then skip separators, return total distance) that
  matches the behavior `tests/test_path.c`'s `test_path_skips` expects --
  the active version looks like a botched UTF-8-aware rewrite of it.

- fmt-escapecharhtml-undeclared: `fmt_escapecharhtml()` is defined (as a
  GNU `__attribute__((alias))` to `fmt_escapecharxml` in
  `lib/fmt/fmt_escapecharxml.c`, or directly in
  `lib/fmt/fmt_escapecharhtml.c` for non-GNUC compilers) but is never
  declared in `lib/fmt.h`, unlike every other `fmt_*` function. Any
  caller outside the `fmt` module needs its own forward declaration to
  use it without an implicit-declaration warning/error. Found while
  writing `tests/test_fmt.c`.

- iom-epoll-kqueue-never-enabled: `lib/iom/*.c` (`iom_init`, `iom_add`,
  `iom_wait`) is written entirely against `HAVE_EPOLL`/`HAVE_KQUEUE`, but
  neither macro is ever defined by the build: the enabling line in
  `lib/io_internal.h` (`//# define HAVE_EPOLL 1`) is commented out, the
  `USE_EPOLL` option in the top-level `CMakeLists.txt` sits inside a
  `[[...]]`-bracket comment (so it isn't even a live `option()`), and the
  `check_function_exists(epoll_wait ...)` / `add_definitions(-DHAVE_EPOLL=1)`
  detection logic right below it is commented out with `#` too. With
  neither macro defined: `iom_init()` never assigns `c->ctx`, so its
  `return (c->ctx != -1)` reads an uninitialized field; `iom_add()` falls
  off the end of a non-`void` function with no `return` at all (the
  `#else` arm is just a `#warning`); and `iom_wait()`'s "we're the thread
  responsible for polling" branch skips the actual `epoll_wait`/`kevent`
  call entirely and falls straight through to `sem_post(&c->sem); return
  1;`, reporting a fake ready event without ever touching `*s`/`*revents`.
  `iom` also isn't in `lib/CMakeLists.txt`'s `MODSUBDIRS` list at all, so
  none of this currently gets built by anything. Found while writing
  `tests/test_iom.c`; left `iom_add`/`iom_wait` untested (calling them
  would exercise undefined behavior) with only `iom_init`/`iom_abort`
  covered, pending someone deciding whether to wire up epoll support for
  real.

- str-str-unimplemented: `str_str()` is declared in `lib/str.h` (with
  `__pure__`) but has no implementation anywhere in the tree -- no
  `lib/str/str_str.c`, no inline definition. Any caller would fail to
  link. Found while writing `tests/test_str.c`; left that test as an
  empty stub rather than calling a function that doesn't exist.

- str-decamelize-unimplemented: `str_decamelize()` is declared in
  `lib/str.h` but has no implementation anywhere in the tree -- no
  `lib/str/str_decamelize.c`, no inline definition. Any caller would fail
  to link. Found while writing `tests/test_str.c`; left that test as an
  empty stub rather than calling a function that doesn't exist.

- mmap-anon-unimplemented: `mmap_anon()` is declared in `lib/mmap.h` but
  has no implementation anywhere in the tree -- no `lib/mmap/mmap_anon.c`,
  no inline definition. Any caller would fail to link. Found while
  writing `tests/test_mmap.c`; left that test as an empty stub rather
  than calling a function that doesn't exist.

- env-unset-desyncs-env-init-bookkeeping: `env_unset()` (`lib/env/env_unset.c`)
  is a thin wrapper around plain `unsetenv()`, but the rest of the `env`
  module (`env_put`/`env_put2`/`env_putb` and friends, in
  `lib/env/env_put.c`) manages `environ` itself once `env_init()` has run:
  it tracks the array's used/allocated slot counts in file-static `en`
  and `ea`, and `env_add()` relies on those counts staying accurate to
  decide whether to grow the array before appending (`environ[en++] = s;
  environ[en] = 0;`). Calling `env_unset()` after `env_init()` has already
  taken over `environ` shrinks the array via `unsetenv()` without telling
  `env_add()`, so `en` ends up one too high. The next `env_put*()` call
  then writes past the array's real last element, silently dropping the
  NUL terminator that every `for(e = environ; *e; e++)` scan (`env_get()`,
  `env_get_b()`, etc.) depends on to stop -- causing an out-of-bounds read
  and segfault soon after. Found while writing `tests/test_env.c`; worked
  around by running the `env_unset()` test before any test that triggers
  `env_init()`, and using plain `setenv()` (not `env_set()`) to seed the
  variable it removes.

- str-triml-inverted-condition: `str_triml()` (`lib/str/str_triml.c`)
  breaks out of its skip loop with `if(byte_chr(charset, charsetlen, *s)
  != charsetlen) break;` -- that condition is true (and so breaks)
  precisely when `*s` *is* found in `charset`, i.e. on the very first
  character a caller wants trimmed. Its sibling `byte_triml()`
  (`lib/byte/byte_triml.c`) has the correct, opposite condition (`==
  charsetlen`, i.e. stop when the character is *not* in charset, keep
  skipping while it is). As written, `str_triml(s, charset, n)` returns
  `s` unchanged whenever `s[0]` is one of the characters to trim. Found
  while writing `tests/test_str.c`; the test documents the current
  (no-op) behavior instead of the intended trim.

- dir-size-windows-only: `dir_size()` (`lib/dir/dir_size.c`) initializes
  its result to -1 and only ever overwrites it inside `#if !USE_READDIR
  && WINDOWS`. On POSIX builds (`USE_READDIR`, i.e. anything using
  `opendir`/`readdir`), that branch never compiles in, so `dir_size()`
  unconditionally returns -1 regardless of the entry's actual size --
  there is no `fstatat`/`stat`-based fallback for the readdir path. Found
  while writing `tests/test_dir.c`; the test only checks the Windows
  branch is skipped (`dir_size` returns -1) rather than asserting an
  actual size on this platform.

- dir-path-missing-directory-prefix: `dir_path()` (`lib/dir/dir_path.c`)
  is supposed to build the full path of the current directory entry into
  `sa`, but on the `USE_READDIR` (POSIX) branch the only line that would
  populate the directory portion is commented out (`// stralloc_copys(sa,
  dh->__d_dirname);`). As written, `dir_path()` on Linux/POSIX appends
  just the entry's basename (via `dir_NAME(d)`) to whatever `sa` already
  contained -- the directory the entry lives in never gets written, so
  `sa` ends up holding only the filename instead of `dir/file`. Found
  while writing `tests/test_dir.c`; the test asserts the current
  (basename-only) result rather than the intended full path.

- taia-fmtfrac-unimplemented: `taia_fmtfrac()` is declared in
  `lib/taia.h` but has no implementation anywhere in the tree -- no
  `lib/taia/taia_fmtfrac.c`, no inline definition. Any caller would fail
  to link. Found while writing `tests/test_taia.c`; left that test as an
  empty stub rather than calling a function that doesn't exist.

- str-istr-first-char-case-sensitive: `str_istr()` in
  `lib/str/str_istr.c` is meant to be a case-insensitive substring search
  (it compares the remainder of the needle with `str_case_diffn`), but its
  first-character check (`while(sc != c)`) compares raw bytes instead of
  folding case. So `str_istr("Hello World", "world")` returns `NULL`
  instead of finding `"World"`, while `str_istr("Hello World", "World")`
  (matching first-letter case) works. Found while writing
  `tests/test_str.c`; the test documents the current (buggy) behavior
  instead of asserting the case-insensitive result a caller would expect.

- genmakefile-objdir-flag-unused: `-R`/`--objdir` is parsed in `main()`'s
  getopt loop (`case 'R': objdir = arg; break;`) but the `objdir` local is
  never read anywhere afterward -- the flag silently accepts a value and
  does nothing with it. Also missing from the `-h` usage text. Not fixed.

- genmakefile-sources2-leak: in `generate_all_rules()` (`genmakefile.c`,
  factored out of `main()`'s former `if(!infile) {...}` block),
  `sources2` (a `strarray` copy of the discovered source list, iterated
  for `sourcedir_addsource()`) is never freed -- a leak on every run that
  doesn't use `-f/--input-file`. Present in the original inline code
  before the refactor too; not fixed (preserved as-is to keep the
  refactor behavior-identical).

- genmakefile-getopt-optstring-duplicate-and-unhandled: `main()`'s
  `unix_getopt_long()` optstring
  (`"ha:bo:O:B:E:d:t:m:n:D:l:I:c:s:p:P:R:S:if:CW:w:L:O:T:X:x:"`) lists
  `O:` twice and declares `E:` (an option requiring an argument) with no
  corresponding `case 'E':` or long-option mapping -- passing `-E foo`
  consumes an argument via getopt and then falls through to `default:`
  ("No such option"). Not fixed.

- genmakefile-lcc-dead-link-command: `set_compiler_type()`'s `lcc` branch
  (`genmakefile.c`, ~line 951) assigns `commands.link` twice in a row with
  no condition between them -- the first `stralloc_copys(&commands.link,
  "$(CC) ... -o $@ $^ ...")` is unconditionally overwritten by the second
  (`"$(LINK) ..."`) immediately after. The first assignment is a dead
  store. Not fixed.

- genmakefile-srcdir-lib-rule-dead-dep-check: `generate_srcdir_lib_rule()`'s
  non-gmake/PIC branch (`src/genmakefile/generate.c`, ~line 501) does
  `dep = generate_simple_compile_rules(...); if(dep) array_catb(&objs,
  &dep, sizeof(target*));` but `generate_simple_compile_rules()`'s only
  `return` statement is `return 0;` (generate.c:463) -- `dep` is always
  NULL here, so the `if(dep)` never fires and this path always falls
  through to the plain `else` branch below it in the caller instead of
  getting the same objs/rule->objs multi-file-recipe treatment the
  gmake/non-PIC path (`generate_srcdir_compile_rules()`) gets. Not fixed.

- mediathek-list-header-row-trailing-garbage: the header record's last field
  (`"neu"`) consistently gets a few bytes of binary garbage appended after it,
  e.g. `"neu ;\x60^\xe4}"` -- observed identically shaped (though not
  byte-identical) across independent runs, and *only* on the first record
  mediathek-list ever emits in a run. Confirmed no longer a symptom of
  `output_entry()`'s escaping (fixed -- see the fixed-bugs note below) since
  it survives with clean escaping and reproduces on every fresh `-u` fetch.
  Most likely cause given it's isolated to the very first record: `char
  buf2[BUFSIZE]` in `parse_mediathek_list()` (mediathek-list.c:842, a
  stack-local array with no initializer) getting a few bytes of leftover
  uninitialized stack memory included past the true end of the first
  record's content, from an off-by-a-few miscount in the record-assembly
  loop above it (mediathek-list.c:882-907, the `buffer_get_token`/
  `buffer_get` extend-until-`"],`-heuristic loop) that only manifests on the
  first iteration (before `prev`/state from a previous record could mask
  it). Not fixed -- would need instrumenting that loop's byte counts against
  the first record specifically to confirm.

  (Fixed, for the record: `output_entry()` not escaping backslashes or
  control characters, and `split_fields()`/`push_unescaped()` not resolving
  the upstream data's own `\"` escapes before re-emitting them -- see git
  history for `mediathek-list.c`. That combination was producing >25% of all
  entries with mis-split, spliced-in-from-a-different-record fields whenever
  a Beschreibung contained a quoted phrase or an embedded literal newline;
  confirmed down to <0.1% (residual is truncation artifacts, not this tool)
  against live fetches after the fix.)

- genmakefile-vars-map-insert-undersized (fixed, for the record):
  `var_list()` (`src/genmakefile/var.c:92`) inserted a `var_t var;`
  local (`{ strlist value; uint32 serial; }`) into the `vars` map via
  `MAP_INSERT2(vars, name, ..., &var, sizeof(strlist))` -- sizing the
  copy by `sizeof(strlist)` instead of `sizeof(var_t)`, silently
  dropping the trailing `serial` field from what actually got
  allocated/copied by the hashmap. Any later read of `var->serial`
  (e.g. `output_var()`, `src/genmakefile/output.c:60`) was an
  out-of-bounds heap read. Caught by AddressSanitizer
  (`heap-buffer-overflow ... READ of size 4 ... 0 bytes after 33-byte
  region`, in `output_var` via `var_set`/`set_compiler_type`/
  `resolve_toolchain`). Fixed by inserting `sizeof(var_t)` instead.

  This was one of six real memory-safety bugs (see git history for the
  fixes, all found via AddressSanitizer against a real 6066-line/4.3MB
  `exec-intercept.c` build log fed through `genmakefile -f`) that
  together were the actual cause of a since-superseded bug report in
  this file (`genmakefile-dual-root-source-path-explosion`) that
  guessed the wrong root cause: apparent non-deterministic 0.7s-vs-60s
  runtime on identical input, believed at the time to be a `qsort`
  comparator blowup from un-canonicalized dual-mount source paths. The
  real cause was `path_collapse()` (`lib/path/path_collapse.c`)
  underflowing a `size_t` index (`x[--i]` with no `i > 0` guard, and a
  separate `n = i + (n - l)` underflow when a collapsed `..` sat at the
  very end of the string) and reading/writing adjacent heap memory --
  ASLR-dependent heap layout is exactly why the timing was
  non-deterministic on identical input. That, plus a heap-use-after-
  free in `input_process_path_b()` (`src/genmakefile/input.c`, the
  relative-path branch passed `out->s` as `path_relative_to()`'s
  source while `out` was also its destination, so the source pointer
  went stale mid-call when the destination reallocated), plus three
  `x + 3`-past-a-2-byte-token overreads in `-D`/`-I`/`-L`/`-MF`
  argument parsing (should have advanced to the next argv token via
  `*++p`, matching the already-correct `-o` handling a few lines
  above), plus a `stralloc_copys()` call on a non-NUL-terminated
  `path_basename2()` slice into a `strlist`'s packed buffer, plus this
  `var_t`/`sizeof(strlist)` bug -- fed each other via corrupted,
  wildly-long strings landing in `sources_set`, which is what actually
  blew up the sort. Verified fixed: 20/20 repeated runs against the
  real log now complete in ~1.7-2.0s with identical (order aside)
  output, and a full AddressSanitizer pass (leak detection aside --
  see below) is clean.

- genmakefile-path-collapse-strlist-at-u8len-chain (fixed, for the record):
  a follow-up investigation into a missing-leading-slash bug in generated
  Makefile paths (`-f exec.log1 -O withdirs -d withdirs/build`, ASan build)
  found a chain of three further real bugs downstream of each other:

  1. `path_collapse()` (`lib/path/path_collapse.c`) cancelled a "real"
     path component against a following `..` without checking whether the
     component being eliminated was itself `.`/`..` -- so a leading run
     of N `..` components (e.g. `"../../foo"`) got collapsed pairwise
     instead of surviving intact, wrongly eating path segments that must
     stay put for `../`-relative output to be correct.

  2. `strlist_at(sl, i)` (`lib/strlist/strlist_at.c`) mishandled the
     "one past the last item" boundary query (used by e.g.
     `strlist_range()`'s exclusive upper bound): it compared the
     separator count `l` directly against `i` (`if(l == i) return ...`)
     instead of `l + 1 == i` (separator count is `item_count - 1`, not
     `item_count`), so querying one past the end returned NULL/garbage
     instead of the one-past-the-end pointer. This fed a `dirs.out.sa`
     stralloc whose `.len` field claimed 4096 bytes when only ~113 were
     real, into...

  3. Six `lib/path/*.c` functions (`path_skip_component`, `path_getsep`,
     `path_len`, `path_len_s`, `path_skips` (first loop), `path_trim_b`)
     looping `for(i = ...; i += len)` with `len = u8_len(&s[i], 1)` and no
     guard against `u8_len()` returning 0 -- which it does on any byte
     that isn't a valid UTF-8 lead byte, e.g. the ASan-poisoned `0xBE`
     garbage past bug 2's phantom `.len`. `i` never advanced, looping
     forever and driving RSS up without bound. Caught live via gdb (FIFO-
     stdin-attach technique, since gdb needs to be the direct parent for
     `ptrace_scope=1` to allow the attach) on the hung process, which
     showed `path_skip_component()` spinning with `len=0` at a fixed `i`.
     Fixed all six with `if((len = u8_len(...)) == 0) len = 1;` (treat an
     invalid lead byte as one raw byte and keep advancing).

  A related but more severe bug was found and fixed in the same audit:
  `path_quote_spaces_b()`/`path_quote_spaces()` (`lib/path/path_quote_spaces_b.c`,
  `path_quote_spaces.c`) never incremented their loop index at all --
  `i`/`len` were computed but `i` was never advanced by `len`, so the
  loop spun forever on the first character of *any* non-space-starting
  input of length > 0, valid UTF-8 or not. Fixed by adding `i += len ?
  len : 1;` at the end of each loop body.

  `path_skip_separator.c`, `path_skip.c`, and `path_is_separator.c` were
  audited too and found not to need a fix: the first two only ever break
  out or return via a condition that's already false-safe when `len==0`
  (an invalid lead byte, always >= 0x80, can never equal a literal
  separator byte, always < 0x80), and the third has no loop at all.

  Verified: ASan build clean (no crash/hang, `halt_on_error=1`) across
  10/10 repeated runs of the original repro (`exec.log1 -O . -d build`)
  and one full pass of the real 6066-line `exec.log`; release build
  clean and stable across 20/20 repeated runs of the repro and 5/5 runs
  of the full log (~1.7-1.8s each, identical output); generated Makefile
  paths spot-checked for the originally-reported missing-leading-slash
  symptom -- none found, all absolute-source paths now correctly
  `../`-prefixed with no dropped separator.

- genmakefile-exits-without-freeing (not fixed, low priority): the
  same AddressSanitizer run above reports ~344MB across ~93k
  allocations leaked at exit against the 4.3MB log (mostly `set_add`/
  `rule_get`-owned structures, `lib/set/set_init.c` and
  `src/genmakefile/rule.c:36`). All bounded/proportional to input size
  (not unbounded growth), and the process exits shortly after, so the
  OS reclaims it regardless -- unlike the bugs above, this is ordinary
  "didn't bother free()ing before a short-lived CLI tool exits"
  hygiene, not a correctness or stability issue. Not worth chasing
  unless `genmakefile` is ever used as a long-lived/embedded library
  rather than a one-shot CLI.
